EXT. BORDER CHECKPOINT - DUSTY ROAD - DAY

A single wooden gate. A booth. A flag that has given up. Heat shimmer over the gravel.

MARSHAL ELIAS CROWE, 50s, dust on his hat, leans out of the booth. Before him, on a mule, sits ROSALIND BEAUMONT, 40s, rifle across her lap, face like closed shutters.

CROWE
Papers.

ROSALIND
Ain't got none.

CROWE
Then you ain't crossing.

ROSALIND
(beat)
You know what the last man who said that to me said after?

CROWE
I'd wager nothing.

ROSALIND
Nothing. That's right. Funny how that works.

Crowe's phone RINGS in the booth. An old landline, cord and all. He lifts it.

CROWE
(into receiver)
Checkpoint.

SHERIFF (V.O.)
(crackling)
Elias, hold her. Don't let her through. She's got a warrant out in three counties.

Crowe looks at Rosalind. She hasn't moved. He lowers the receiver slowly.

CROWE
(to her)
Sheriff says I should hold you.

ROSALIND
(softly)
And what do you say?

CROWE
(long beat)
I say it's hot. And I'm tired. And in thirty years nobody ever asked me what I wanted. Not once.

He hangs up the phone. Opens the gate.

CROWE (CONT'D)
Go on. Before I think better of it.

ROSALIND
(touching her hat)
You'll regret it.

CROWE
Probably. That's what the gate's for.

She clicks her tongue. The mule crosses. Dust rises. Crowe watches until she is a speck, then picks the receiver up again, and tells the sheriff she never came.
